+
+# Tags on posts
+
+## Context
+
+The posts list is one long feed and readers cannot find posts on a topic.
+Product asked for author-written tags, shown wherever a post appears, with
+a click-through to the list filtered by that tag. Topic pages and a tag
+cloud will build on the same data, so the storage shape is a contract.
+
+## Decision
+
+- Two tables: `tags` (`id`, `name` unique, case-sensitive) and `post_tags`
+  (`post_id`, `tag_id`, composite primary key). `Post.belongsToMany('tags')`
+  reads them; the `PostTag` pivot model writes them, since the ORM has no
+  attach/detach/sync helpers.
+- The form field `tags` is one comma-separated string (a string list is
+  accepted too). `parseTagNames()` in the validator trims, drops empties and
+  collapses case-sensitive duplicates; then each name is 1–30 characters and
+  a post carries at most 5. Violations are a 422 on the `tags` field, like an
+  empty title, and nothing is written.
+- Saving replaces the post's tag set inside one transaction: unknown names
+  become new tags, missing links are added, dropped links are removed. An
+  unused tag row stays.
+- `PostResource` always carries `tags: string[]`, sorted alphabetically
+  (`[]` when none). Every action that renders posts eager-loads `tags`.
+- `/posts?tag=<name>` filters by pivot ids; the paginator carries the `tag`
+  query so page links keep the filter, and an unknown tag is an empty page.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Topic pages can read `tags` / `post_tags` directly and reuse
+  `PostResource`.
+- Deleting a post removes its pivot rows explicitly (SQLite enforces the
+  cascade only with the foreign-key pragma on).
+- The route body type for `tags` is its input shape, so the controller
+  re-runs `parseTagNames()` on the validated value.
